-- Issue 1139, weaker consequence (3) — a DIVERGING call in a non-final
-- constructor argument.  `boom ()` is an application, hence expansive, and it
-- sits in NON-final position of `PA _ _`.  Before the fix only the final
-- argument (`[]`) was tested, so `p` generalized to `PA a` and could be
-- consumed at `PA Int` AND `PA String` in the same program.
--
-- WHY TWO TYPES: see reject_1139_nonfinal_ctor_effect.mdk.  A one-type use
-- cannot distinguish fixed from broken.  Note also that the free type variable
-- is load-bearing — it comes from the empty list `[]`.  Give the binding a
-- ground type (`data PA a = PA Int a`, `PA (boom ()) True`) and generalization
-- is vacuous, so the fixture would pass for the wrong reason.
--
-- Verdict: REJECT, by a type-mismatch DIAGNOSTIC — specifically NOT by the
-- panic.  The gate's Bug #40 check enforces that distinction: if the program
-- were still admitted, `run` would report E-PANIC instead, which is graded
-- RANPANIC and fails even though the exit code is 1 either way.
data PA a = PA Unit (List a)

boom : Unit -> Unit
boom _ = panic "BOOM"

useI : PA Int -> Int
useI (PA _ xs) = length xs

useS : PA String -> Int
useS (PA _ xs) = length xs

main =
  let p = PA (boom ()) []
  println (debug (useI p + useS p))
